module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_coproduct_arm_without_a_constructor_name_is_accepted

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data a_coproduct_arm_without_a_constructor_name_is_accepted: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_coproduct_arm_without_a_constructor_name_is_accepted" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a coproduct declaration with an arm that has no constructor name, `type T = { x: Int } | B`. Nothing can build or match that arm, so the declaration is malformed. v2 normalize accepts it, and so does v2 resolve on the single-tree door. Measured by claim_batch on BuildBuddy, 2026-10-05 (clever-raven-680): `v2.test.claim.long.cwc_resolution_probe` `cwc_malformed_nary_coproduct_normalize_rejects` FAILs. The claim hands normalize a supplied parse tree for `type T = { x: Int } | B`; the module's real-path claim `cwc_cwc_module_resolve_accepts` checks that the real parser emits that tree for the source, by content hash.",
    "HOW IT HID: the witness carried this claim beside its own negation (`cwc_malformed_nary_coproduct_fail_open_control`, `!cwc_norm_rejects` over the same source). Both became `test fn` in gunbc#7287; at gunbc#7002 the negation was a helper inside one conjunction. So one of the pair was red whatever normalize did, and the file read as a stale shape rather than a finding. The negation is deleted and the remaining claim discriminates. Until gunbc#13333 the module was frozen in `gunbc.witness.witness_deferral_freeze`, so the floor never ran it. That PR retired the freeze row and enrolled this claim in `v2.workflow.floor_expected_red`, so the required floor now runs it and holds its failure as agreement.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor (silent acceptance of a malformed declaration). CEILING: structurally impossible. The arm's children are in hand when the Disj node is built, so the fold can refuse an arm with no name. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: the declaration fold that builds a coproduct refuses an arm with no authored constructor name, with a located diagnostic, and the claim above turns green. Owner unassigned at filing. The claim is enrolled in `v2.workflow.floor_expected_red` (`floor_expected_red_chunk_coproduct_arm_without_constructor_name`); when it turns green the roster row is deleted and the claim stays as the control.",
    "CLIMB RECEIPT (gunbc#13745, tidy-owl-279): the trigger landed. The coproduct-building fold `v2.std.compilers.sugar.sugar_variant_to_disj_edge` refuses an arm whose type body is written directly as a `field_decl_block` or `fn_type` production -- the two `type_expr` branches (grammar: `type_expr = choice(qualified_type, fn_type, field_decl_block)`) that spell no constructor name -- decided from the arm's grammar position, spelling-agnostic, with a located diagnostic. The spelling-agnostic reading is pinned by an executed positive control in the same test: `cwc_named_lbrace_coproduct_normalize_accepts`, a tokenize -> parse -> normalize over `type T = dag_token_lbrace | B`, a legitimate coproduct whose first constructor is NAMED `dag_token_lbrace` (a spelling that is also a lex token class) and must normalize; the earlier spelling-based gate mis-refused it. The negative control `cwc_malformed_nary_coproduct_normalize_rejects` (over `{ x: Int } | B`) turned green. Second rung, same wall: a where clause rebuilt the arm before the fold saw it. The where branch of body lowering (`v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold.body_lower_type_variant`) rebuilds a where-suffix arm into a synthetic conj carrying the lowered head under `^dag_surface_type_expr`, so the raw `seq(type_expr, suffix)` shape -- and with it the production-identity reading -- is gone by the time the sugar fold runs; the refusal then reads the CARRIED HEAD's kind, the lowered image of the same production identity (named head: `Atom`, a dotted qualified-name spine `Conj`, or an `Instantiation`/`Occupancy` wrap; nameless head: a record `Conj` from a field block or an `Arrow` from a fn type). A nameless arm with a where suffix (`type T = { x: Int } where brand(\"X\") | B`) is refused, pinned by the real-path negative control `cwc_nameless_where_coproduct_normalize_rejects`; a NAMED head with the same where clause still normalizes, pinned by the paired positive `cwc_named_where_coproduct_normalize_accepts`, so the refusal is keyed to the head being a nameless type body, never to the presence of a where clause. A head the rebuild hands over that none of the named kinds can read is refused -- the where-rebuilt shape never fails open. Third rung, same wall: the spine check was label-only. `qualified_name_spine_shape_present` verifies a two-child `Conj` has the spine's head/tail LABELS, not that the targets are a name spine; a record whose field NAMES are spelled like the spine labels (`fold_list_node_head` / `fold_list_node_tail`) passed as a dotted name and the arm normalized (`type T = { fold_list_node_head: Int, fold_list_node_tail: Int } where brand(\"X\") | B`). The carried `Conj` head is now named only when the full qualified-name reader (`v2.std.qualified_name.qualified_name_from_node`) reads it to a SUCCESSFUL NONEMPTY name: its fold validates the spine's targets (head edge targets an atom, tail edge a spine conj), which a record payload fails; a successful read of the reader's empty name and a rejected read both count as nameless. Pinned by the real-path negative control `cwc_spine_named_fields_where_coproduct_normalize_rejects`; dotted names (`a.B`, `a.b.C`) still normalize through the same reader. The `floor_expected_red` enrollment (`floor_expected_red_chunk_coproduct_arm_without_constructor_name` and its roster link) is deleted with the green flip, and the claims stay as the permanent regression controls. Production handling (the silent acceptance) is discharged; this row is the evidence and stays updated.",
  ],
  evidence: [],
}
